# C512 fusion: use the native stage boundaries and enough independent CTAs

A direct `C=512` extension of the C256 whole-window kernel is not a useful first
experiment. It changes the FFN architecture, exceeds the naive register budget,
and launches too few independent windows. A bounded QKV-plus-attention stage
with four heads per CTA is the practical next experiment. This is a CPU-only
feasibility analysis; no speed or roofline gain is inferred.

## The arithmetic that must remain

C512 first computes a dense 512→512 W1 and publishes E4 bytes. It then executes
eight 64→256→64 branches, with cubic Half arithmetic and E4 publications after
expansion and contraction. W4 is a second dense 512→512, initialized with the
Half-rounded product of the original published input and FFN scale. Published
FFN feeds the dense 512→1536 QKV. Sixteen independent 32-channel heads use64-token
shifted windows. The final dense 512→512 is initialized from published FFN times
the attention scale; block30 also retains raw Half output for pooling.

The C256 expert block instead has grouped full-input256→128→32 branches before
its dense projection. Merely increasing its channel template silently implements
the wrong C512 FFN. Every C512 dense/branch accumulation is one ascending K chain;
none of the global C1024 partition/reassociation rules apply.

## Measured hardware and compiled resource evidence

The saved SM120 NCU device attributes report 188 SMs,65,536 registers per block
and per SM,255 registers per thread,1,024 threads per block,1,536 threads/48 warps
per SM,49,152 default shared bytes per block,101,376 opt-in shared bytes per
block,102,400 shared bytes per SM and 1,024 reserved shared bytes per CTA.
These values come from
`profile/window-vector-weights-fp8-b15-136x240-p1-v3-20261003T050345_438229Z/analysis/full_metrics.json`,
not assumptions about SM100/B200.

The existing rolled C256 vector-cache kernel uses 168 registers at 256 threads
and 16,384 shared bytes. Keeping that per-thread footprint while expanding to
512 threads would require86,016 registers, above the 65,536-register block limit.
A 512-thread design needs no more than128 allocated registers per thread to
fit one CTA, accounting for allocation granularity. Simply forcing a register
cap could spill and is not a performance solution.

Read-only `cuobjdump --dump-resource-usage` on the original pinned
`assets/vendor_modules/module_4.cubin` confirms these actual resources:

| Native stage | Threads | Registers/thread | Shared bytes | Grid structure |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| W1 + branches | 256 | 128 | 9,232 | Two CTAs per unshifted8×8 window |
| W4 + residual | 128 | 128 | 13,336 | Twice the horizontal8-pixel tile count |
| QKV + attention | 128 | 168 | 9,232 | Four CTAs per shifted8×8 window |
| Projection + residual | 256 | 102 | 13,336 | Twice the horizontal8-pixel tile count |

All four original functions report zero stack and local bytes. The resource
extract is saved in `outputs/c512_native_resource_audit.json.draft`, pinned to
cubin SHA256 `97bdf9a571dec8bff41975fe670610b0a3363b49885ada171e61229578ecf569`.
Input/output-view and pooled-output variants have different launches and are
not conflated with this ordinary four-stage contract.

The PTX supports the deliberate ownership split. Native branch weight addressing
uses `(tid.y & 3)*64 + ctaid.z*256`; the later branch index is
`(tid.y & 3) + ctaid.z*4`. QKV uses `ctaid.z*384 + tid.y*96`, exactly four
96-channel Q/K/V head groups per CTA. QKV's128 threads are four head warps.
Its declared shared input is8,192 bytes plus16 bytes of copy barriers; compiled
shared usage is9,232. Both native stages contain bulk global-to-shared copies
and mbarrier synchronization. The smaller native staging allocation is evidence
of a streamed design; copying its entire asynchronous protocol requires a
separate address/order proof, not a mechanical source translation.

## Why one CTA for the whole window is a poor starting point

Phase0 has18,40 and 135 C512 windows at the 720p,1080p and 4K anchors. At4K the
other phases have 135 or144 windows. One CTA per window therefore cannot occupy
all 188 SMs even if resources were free. Four CTAs per window produce 72,160
and 540 phase0 CTAs respectively, matching the native QKV decomposition.
The small anchors can still underfill the device; the 85% utilization target is
not automatically achievable for every small isolated workload.

A 256-thread whole-window variant could process two heads per warp sequentially
and use two 32KiB shared exchange pages to keep FFN and attention alive. Its 64KiB
allocation would need explicit opt-in, limit residency, and still leave only
one CTA per window. A 512-thread version with one head per warp would need to
shorten register lifetimes substantially to fit the block limit. Neither solves
the independent-window count.

Splitting final query rows across several whole-block CTAs would require each
CTA to recompute full-window FFN and K/V, because all 64 keys contribute to every
query row. Splitting output channels still requires all attention heads for the
final dense projection. That duplication is an arithmetic cost, not a free way
to increase occupancy. We should retain global stage boundaries where they
avoid duplicated work and provide independent CTA ownership.

## First private prototype: four-head QKV plus attention

Use128 threads per CTA and grid `(window, head_group)`, where `head_group=0..3`.
Warp `w` owns head `4*head_group+w`, all 64 window tokens and 32 channels. Reuse
the existing canonical vector B-cache layout for QKV `[1536,512]`; each warp's
matrix row begins at `head*96 + kind*32`. All16 ascending K32 steps remain in
their original order. The cache contains the same bytes and does not rotate K.

The first version can allocate one read-only `Exchange<512>` of 32,768 bytes.
The four warps cooperatively fill its 16 channel groups: warp `w` loads groups
`w, w+4, w+8, w+12`, for all four M16 tiles. Each exchange element has one
writer, followed by one unconditional CTA barrier. Every head warp then reads
the same immutable input. This avoids introducing an asynchronous shared-memory
pipeline before correctness and actual resource costs are established.

After QKV, keep the existing Q/K Half normalization tree, Q head-scale rounding,
E4 publication, V transpose, physical token order, bias addressing, bit-affine
exponential, Half denominator tree/floor/reciprocal, E4 probabilities and PV K32
order. Output packed BHWC directly. A Half-storage diagnostic output, if added,
must decode the same published E4 values rather than expose un-published PV.
Packed output can feed the existing residual operator directly, removing its
current attention Half-to-E4 input repack without changing arithmetic.

Publication needs no cross-head exchange: each warp scatters only its own head.
For a valid logical `(y,x)`, the shifted-window inverse is uniquely
`wx=floor((x+sx)/8)`, `wy=floor((y+sy)/8)`, and the token within that window is
unique. Head groups partition all 16 heads. Thus valid `(batch,y,x,channel)`
outputs have one writer; invalid padded tokens execute warp-wide shuffles but
perform no global store. The implementation must prove that ownership against
the existing physical-token helper for every phase and four-aligned tail.

At the native168-register footprint,128 threads require21,504 registers;
three such CTAs would require64,512 registers. Three32KiB exchanges plus the
reported reserved shared bytes total101,376 bytes, just below the reported
102,400-byte SM limit. This is arithmetic feasibility only: compiled register
allocation, carveout and actual occupancy must be measured. The new kernel's
register count is unknown until compilation; it must not inherit a guessed
168-register claim from the native implementation.

This stage currently costs about31.34us QKV plus18.64us attention per block in
the saved eager v29 trace. Fusing it can avoid materializing the 25,067,520-byte
Half QKV tensor at M8160, but reduced traffic is not a speed estimate. The
existing QKV GEMM reuses weights over larger M tiles; the fused M64/head-local
schedule may trade some of that reuse for less temporary traffic. Benchmark it
against the selected public QKV-plus-attention route, including the final
publication and matched output storage, before considering public dispatch.

The alternative small prototype is W1 plus the independent branches, leaving W4
as its current residual GEMM. A warp can own one 64-channel branch and M16 rows:
16 W1 accumulator words, eight published input words, streamed32-channel hidden
chunks and 16 contraction accumulator words. Two/four/eight branch warps per CTA
can vary independent CTA count without duplicating matrix arithmetic. That
proposal remains secondary until the current QKV-attention experiment is measured.

No new kernel has been compiled or executed by this feasibility audit. All
resource and native-ownership claims above are bounded to saved artifacts.
